'''Shiga Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|滋賀県}}, ''Shiga-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the western coast of the island of [[Ishijima]], facing the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the west. Its population as of 2026 was 1,305,296, with approximately 38% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Shiga, Shiga|Shiga]] (pop. 532,088). Shiga Prefecture occupies a strategic position on the Ishijima western seaboard, where the [[Katsuran Sea]] provides a sheltered maritime corridor connecting the Ishijima industrial belt to the trade routes of the Shendan Ocean and the broader Fosian market.

Shiga is defined by its relationship with the sea. The [[Katsuran Sea]]—a partially enclosed body of water between the western coast of Ishijima and the open Shendan—provides calm, deep-water harbours that have made Shiga one of the most important manufacturing and shipping centres in Okaiken. The prefecture's western coastline is a continuous strip of industrial ports, shipyards, and manufacturing facilities, collectively known as the {{nihongo|'''Shiga Manufacturing Corridor'''|滋賀製造回廊|Shiga Seizō Kairō}}, which produces approximately 18% of Ishijima's total industrial output despite containing only 12% of the island's population.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The western coast of Ishijima has been a centre of maritime activity since the earliest periods of settlement. The [[Atsu]] coastal communities of the Shiga shoreline were among the first to develop deep-water fishing techniques, and the sheltered harbours of the Katsuran Sea provided ideal anchorages for the [[Kajika]] wayfinding expansion. By the 6th century CE, the Shiga coast had become a major node in the Shendan trade network, with the [[Port of Shiga]] serving as the primary gateway for goods moving between the Ishijima interior and the western sea routes.

The [[Shiga Domain]] ({{lang|ja|滋賀藩}}, ''Shiga-han'') was established during the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a direct retainer territory of the [[Hoko clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who controlled the western maritime approaches to the Ishijima interior. The domain's wealth was built on its control of the Katsuran Sea fishing grounds and its position as the western terminus of the [[Tochigi Mountain Route]], which carried goods from the landlocked interior to the coast for shipment.

=== Industrialisation and the Shiga shipyards ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Shiga Domain into Shiga Prefecture, and the new government immediately recognised the strategic value of the Katsuran Sea harbours for its industrialisation programme. The [[Shiga Naval Shipyard]], established in 1876, was one of the first state-directed industrial projects of the Restoration era, producing steel-hulled vessels for the modernising [[Sanese Imperial Fleet]].

The shipyard became the nucleus of the Shiga Manufacturing Corridor, attracting a cascade of related industries—steel rolling mills, engine works, rope walks, and sail-making factories—that transformed the western coastline from a fishing and trading coast into a dense industrial zone. By 1910, Shiga Prefecture was producing more steel than any other prefecture on Ishijima except [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]], and the [[Port of Shiga]] had become the island's second-busiest harbour after Aizawa.

=== Post-war manufacturing boom ===
The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period saw the Shiga Manufacturing Corridor diversify from shipbuilding and steel into a broader range of manufacturing industries. The [[Shiga Motors]] plant, established in 1962, became the largest automotive assembly facility on Ishijima, producing over 280,000 vehicles annually at its peak. The [[Shiga Precision Steel Works]], a specialty steel producer, developed high-grade alloy steels for the automotive, aerospace, and semiconductor industries that remain among the most sought-after materials in Fosian manufacturing.

The 1990s Restructuring Era saw the Shiga Corridor pivot from declining heavy industries to advanced manufacturing. The [[Shiga Semiconductor Fabrication Plant]], opened in 1998, was one of the first major semiconductor foundries on Ishijima and is now part of the [[Kizan Systems]] production network. The [[Shiga Marine Robotics Division]], a spin-off from the shipyard, has become a world leader in autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) technology for deep-sea mining and oceanographic research.

== Geography ==

Shiga Prefecture occupies the western coast of central Ishijima, facing the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the west and bordered by [[Tochigi Prefecture|Tochigi]] to the east, [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]] to the south, and [[Gunma Prefecture|Gunma]] to the north. The Katsuran Sea, a partially enclosed body of water between the Ishijima coast and the open Shendan Ocean, provides the prefecture with its defining geographic feature: a sheltered, deep-water coastline that supports the Shiga Manufacturing Corridor and the Port of Shiga.

The prefecture's topography is divided into three distinct zones. The western coastal plain, a narrow strip of low-lying land between the sea and the interior hills, contains the majority of the prefecture's population and industry. The [[Shiga Hills]], a range of low, forested ridges rising to 300–500 m, form a transitional zone between the coast and the interior. The eastern highlands, contiguous with the Asuka Range of neighbouring Tochigi, rise to over 1,000 m and are sparsely populated, supporting forestry and upland agriculture.

The [[Shiga River]] ({{lang|ja|滋賀川}}, ''Shiga-gawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 54 km, flows from the eastern highlands through the Shiga Hills to the coast, providing freshwater for the industrial corridor and supporting the prefecture's remaining agricultural land in the interior valleys.

