== Economy ==

Shiga Prefecture's economy is dominated by the {{nihongo|'''Shiga Manufacturing Corridor'''|滋賀製造回廊|Shiga Seizō Kairō}}, a 45-kilometre strip of industrial facilities, ports, and logistics centres along the Katsuran Sea coast. The Corridor is the second-largest manufacturing zone on Ishijima after the Aizawa shipyard complex, and its output of approximately 円420 billion annually represents roughly 18% of Ishijima's total industrial production.

=== Shipbuilding and marine engineering ===
The [[Shiga Naval Shipyard]], the oldest continuously operating shipyard on Ishijima, remains the prefecture's single largest industrial employer with approximately 6,800 workers. The shipyard produces medium-sized commercial vessels—container ships, LNG carriers, and specialised deep-sea mining support vessels—for the domestic and Fosian markets. The [[Shiga Marine Robotics Division]], a spin-off established in 2004, has become a world leader in autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) technology, producing the [[Katsuran-Class AUV]] used by the [[Sanese Imperial Fleet]] and by civilian deep-sea mining operators across the Shendan.

=== Automotive manufacturing ===
The [[Shiga Motors]] assembly plant in [[Ogaki, Shiga|Ogaki]] is the largest automotive manufacturing facility on Ishijima, producing approximately 280,000 vehicles annually. The plant is a major employer for the western Ishijima corridor, with a supply chain that extends into neighbouring Tochigi and Ehime prefectures. Shiga Motors produces mid-range passenger vehicles and light commercial vehicles for the domestic market, with approximately 30% of output exported to Fosian markets via the Port of Shiga.

=== Specialty steel ===
The [[Shiga Precision Steel Works]], located in [[Towa, Shiga|Towa]], is one of Okaiken's leading producers of high-grade alloy steels. The facility specialises in automotive-grade sheet steel, aerospace alloys, and the ultra-pure silicon steel used in transformer cores and electric motor laminations. The Precision Steel Works is a key supplier to both Shiga Motors and the [[Kizan Systems]] semiconductor foundries, and its specialty alloys are exported to manufacturers across Fosia.

=== Semiconductors ===
The [[Shiga Semiconductor Fabrication Plant]], opened in 1998 in the [[Hoko, Shiga|Hoko]] Industrial Zone, was one of the first major semiconductor foundries on Ishijima. The facility is now operated by [[Kizan Systems]] as part of its national production network, producing advanced logic chips and memory devices. The plant employs approximately 3,200 workers and represents the most technologically advanced manufacturing operation in the prefecture.

=== Maritime logistics ===
The [[Port of Shiga]] is the second-busiest port on Ishijima after [[Aizawa]], handling approximately 42 million tonnes of cargo annually. The port's deep-water berths can accommodate the largest container vessels operating in the Shendan, and its position on the Katsuran Sea provides sheltered access to the western Ishijima market. The [[Shiga Free Trade Zone]], established in 2008, provides tax incentives for import-export businesses and has attracted a cluster of logistics companies, freight forwarders, and customs brokers to the port district.

=== Fishing ===
The Katsuran Sea supports a significant mackerel fishery, and the [[Shiga Mackerel Fleet]]—approximately 120 vessels operating from the ports of [[Susa, Shiga|Susa]] and [[Fukue, Shiga|Fukue]]—lands approximately 28,000 tonnes of saba annually. The fleet is part of the broader Ishijima saba fishery that supplies the national market, and the Shiga mackerel is prized for its high oil content and firm texture, a result of the nutrient-rich upwellings of the Katsuran Sea.
Shiga's manufacturing base has diversified into advanced electronic substrates and freshwater aquaculture technology. Capitalizing on its inland aquatic resources, the prefecture manufactures high-purity ceramic substrates, semiconductor test sockets, and passive heat sinks used in automotive electronics. In coastal lagoons and managed river estuaries, agricultural cooperatives operate automated environmental water-filtration arrays and freshwater finfish hatcheries, supplying fingerlings to mariculture stations across Ishijima while maintaining strict biological standards for industrial effluent.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Shiga Prefecture's cuisine is defined by its maritime industrial culture. The signature dish is {{nihongo|'''Shiga saba-zushi'''|滋賀鯖寿司|Shiga saba-zushi}}, vinegared mackerel pressed with rice in wooden moulds—a preparation that reflects the prefecture's combination of fishing tradition and woodworking skill. The dish is a staple of the port district restaurants and is served at the annual [[Shiga Port Festival]] in August.

Other local specialties include ''Katsuran miso'' (a rich, dark miso paste made with seaweed from the Katsuran Sea), ''Ogaki ramen'' (a pork-bone broth ramen topped with saba fillets—a distinctive combination that reflects the prefecture's dual fishing and industrial heritage), and ''Shiga senbei'' (rice crackers made with Katsuran Sea salt, sold as souvenirs at the port).

=== The Port Festival ===
The [[Shiga Port Festival]] ({{lang|ja|滋賀港祭り}}, ''Shiga Minato Matsuri''), held annually in August, is the largest festival on the western Ishijima coast, drawing over 180,000 visitors. The festival features a parade of decorated cargo vessels through the Port of Shiga, a saba-grilling competition, and a fireworks display over the Katsuran Sea. The festival celebrates the prefecture's maritime industrial heritage and the relationship between the Shiga workforce and the sea.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Shiga Line]] connects [[Shiga Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Aizawa Station]], providing high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The [[Katsuran Coastal Line]], a conventional rail service, runs along the western coast connecting the manufacturing towns of the Corridor.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Port of Shiga]] is the prefecture's primary commercial harbour and the second-busiest port on Ishijima. Regular ferry services connect Shiga to the [[Ogasawara Islands]] and to ports along the Shendan coast. The [[Katsuran Sea Ferry]], a passenger and vehicle service, operates between Shiga and the western Shendan islands.

=== Air ===
Shiga Airport (SGA) serves as the regional hub for domestic flights, with connections to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]].

== Education ==

The [[Shiga Institute of Marine Engineering]] is the prefecture's premier research institution, specialising in naval architecture, marine propulsion systems, and autonomous underwater vehicle technology. The [[Shiga Technical College]], affiliated with the Manufacturing Corridor, trains technicians and engineers for the automotive, steel, and semiconductor industries.

